From 0217306da714a339c39923c885c2fbb9411339c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@ubuntu.localdomain>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:34:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] feat(rlharness): deterministic stage start; G0.2 item 1
 passes over 5,000 frames

Two fixes, both found by instrumenting rather than guessing.

1. Leave the title screen through the title's own code path.  Switching
   g_Supervisor.curState from outside made the title's calc element notice the
   change and ask the chain to exit, so the episode died on the very next pumped
   frame - the reason an episode lasted only ~330 frames.  TitleScreen now calls
   rlharness::ConsumePendingStart() from its Ready state and returns
   CHAIN_CALLBACK_RESULT_CONTINUE_AND_REMOVE_JOB, exactly like the
   developer-autoplay path already does, so the title removes itself.

2. Wait for the stage's own setup, not the title's leftover flag.  RESET accepted
   GAMEPLAY_SETUP_COMPLETE while that flag was still set from the title's own
   initialisation: RESET returned ready=1 with setup_frames=35, curState=1 and no
   setup subthread, and the real setup then ran later inside STEP at a
   scheduler-dependent point.  RESET now requires the state to be observed
   IN_PROGRESS before accepting COMPLETE; setup_frames is ~175 as it should be.

Also fixes two fields that leaked harness artefacts into the hash:
  - stage RNG origin is captured at reset instead of lazily on first HASH (the
    lazy capture made field 15 underflow to 65534);
  - g_ReplayManager->frameCounter is only read while flags.isReplay, because the
    pointer is stale once the replay chain is gone.

And DEBUG now reports the thread count from /proc/self/task: the process runs
with 6-7 threads (SDL/Mesa), which is fine for determinism on one core but
matters for the fork work in WP5, where 10-P0 4.7 requires a single thread.

Result: two processes, same StageStart, same 2,500-chunk input plan, 5,000
frames: 501 hash samples identical, ending hash 1ae73523eb15d77f.  Repeated
twice, four runs in total.  That is 10-P0 WP5 acceptance item 1.
